  "measurementMethod": "Five alternating baseline/candidate pairs for each of two scenarios. The second scenario first observes and closes 96 additional idle sessions, then constructs the identical 96-session/192-incident active workload. Both binaries must durably persist all 384 advancing live ordering timestamps; 384 exact retries must cause no writes or broadcasts. Each run preserves all 192 incidents, including 96 unresolved. Complete durations and every per-run p50/p95/p99/max are retained. Summary acknowledgement percentiles are medians of the five per-run percentiles, not pooled-sample percentiles; per-run tail maxima are retained separately. Percentile indices follow the harness's floor((n-1)*p), with the median for p50. Startup and completion across-run percentiles have only five samples. Fresh processes use normal OS caches on one shared Linux environment; coordinated CPU-heavy jobs were paused during timing. This does not measure Windows disk latency, physical cold caches or peak RSS.",
  "performanceClaim": "No speedup claim. The active-only workload keeps equivalent durable live ordering and full content. With 96 retired sessions, the candidate additionally persists 96 closure fences (18,900 more saved bytes than the baseline); the measured ACK completion cost and all exact-retry timings are reported without treating small timing differences as speedups. The baseline's faster closure acknowledgements lack retired-session replay protection and are not an equivalent closure guarantee. No public limits, workload, incident content, or owner authorization is reduced for these measurements.",
